  "steam"
 ],
 "body": "## Objective\n\nWhat share of e-receipt (mailbox) spend goes to Steam, and how is that share trending year on year? Built as an exploration page for analysts covering PC gaming / Valve-exposed names.\n\n## Data locations\n\n### Inputs\n\nAll read by the `steam` workflow (`workflows/jobs/steam/steam.yaml` in the srs repo), which rebuilds `SRS_MCP.STEAM.*` in full each run.\n\n- **`SRS_SLICE.COMMON.ITEMS_V`** (NIQ Slice, US): Steam lines are `merchant_id = 11730`. Mailbox spend is all merchants except Amazon (`merchant_id = 1`: its parsed volume jumps ~25x from Q4-24 and its receipt format changed in Jul-26). Every mailbox, active or exited.\n- **`SHARE_NIQ.SRS.FOX_TRANSACTION_ITEMS`** (NIQ Fox, UK, Germany, France, Italy, Spain): Steam lines are `merchant_name = 'steam'`. Deduplicated to one row per merchant, order and item (Fox repeats a line per industry tag). Fox restates its history when it refreshes, so EU5 values move slightly between refreshes.\n- Both: daily from 2020-01-01, priced lines only, line value (price x quantity) up to 10,000 in local currency.\n- **Card, US: `CER.CER_1415_MU_DAILY_SIGNAL`** (Consumer Edge MU aggregate, projected US card spend), from 2021-04-01. Steam: `DAY_SYM_BRAND_CHANNEL_ALL` brand 12648 (STEAM). Card spend: `TOTAL_GEOGRAPHY_CHANNEL_DAY` rows with `subindustry_id = 'NA'` and `industry_name = 'ALL'`, summed over every metro area. The DISTRIBUTION channel is left out of both.\n- **Card, EU5: `SHARE_FABLE.FABLE_SIGNAL.{UK,DE,IT,ES}_V2_TXN`, `FR_V3_TXN`**, from 2020-01-01. Consumer accounts (`usertype = 'C'`), debits, `txntype = 'CARD PAYMENT'`, up to 10,000. Steam by card descriptor (STEAMGAMES, STEAM PURCHASE, STEAM GAMES, WL *STEAM, KLARNA*STEAM, VALVE CORP). Fable's srs aggregate has a `steam games` tag but no panel total; summing its tags breaks in 2026, when large merchants lose their tag.\n\n### Method\n\n1. **Steam spend types** (keyword rules in SQL on the lower-cased description, or the item after the last \"|\" for Fox, first match wins): wallet top-ups, hardware, subscriptions, in-game currency / passes / keys, premium editions, DLC / add-ons, games (everything else). Rules are in the job's `load/receipts.sql` (ported from `TYPE_RULES` in the notebook, which lists the largest items per type as a check). Last 12 months, US: games 54%, wallet 18%, in-game 10%, DLC 7%, editions 7%, hardware 4%, subscriptions 0.5%; EU5: games 66%, in-game 14%, DLC 9%, editions 6%, hardware 3%.\n2. **Share** = Steam spend / mailbox spend over a trailing window (1, 7, 28 or 91 days; sums, not an average of daily shares), per country in local currency (no FX).\n3. **Regions.** EU5 = population-weighted average of the five country shares; Combined = population-weighted average of all six (US 335m, UK 68.3m, DE 84.5m, FR 68.4m, IT 59.0m, ES 48.6m). Regions stop at their members' earliest last complete day.\n4. **Y/Y** = value on a day / value 364 days earlier - 1, so weekdays line up. EU5 and Combined Y/Y is of the population-weighted share.\n5. **Quarters** = share over each calendar quarter's days (current quarter to date), Y/Y against the same days shifted back 364 days.\n6. **Last complete day** per country: the latest day, at most three days before the run, whose mailbox spend is at least 75% of the median of the prior 28 days.\n7. **Year chart:** one line per year on a Jan-Dec axis; year k back is shifted forward k x 364 days, so the gap between adjacent lines is the Y/Y. Nominal shows the share, Y/Y the share Y/Y.\n8. **Type tick boxes:** the share and Y/Y use the sum of the ticked types. Default: every type except wallet top-ups (top-ups fund later purchases that send their own receipts, so including them double counts).\n9. **Source toggle:** E-receipt (default) or Card. Card share = Steam card charges / all card spend, same windows, regions, Y/Y and quarters. The last 10 days of each card series are dropped while they settle. Card sees every charge to Steam (wallet top-ups included) but not purchases paid from wallet balance, and has no item detail, so with Card every type is ticked and locked. Card totals include groceries, fuel and bills, so card shares (~0.05-0.1%) sit below mailbox shares; compare the Y/Y. Since Apr 2024 the 91-day share Y/Y correlates 0.81 (US) and 0.68 (EU5) with e-receipts excl. wallet top-ups.\n\n### Outputs\n\n- **`SRS_MCP.STEAM`** tables: `receipt_lines` (Steam lines, unclassified), `receipt_daily` (Steam spend by country, day and type), `mailbox_daily` (mailbox spend by country and day), `card_daily` (Steam and total card spend by country and day). Queryable from Claude chat.\n- **`steam.json`** beside the page: per country, daily arrays of Steam spend for each of the seven types and mailbox spend (local currency) from 2020-01-01 to the country's last complete day, plus population weights.\n- **`card.json`** beside the page: per country, daily Steam and total card spend to its last day less 10 days. If it is missing, the page shows e-receipts only and disables Card.\n- The page computes windows, shares, regions, Y/Y and the quarter table in the browser.\n\n## Routines and manual steps\n\n- **Who and when:** the `steam` srs workflow, daily at 10 AM ET once enabled. It runs only when Slice, Fox, Consumer Edge or Fable has new data.\n- **Isolation:** the receipt tables and the card table load in separate branches. A file whose tables were not rebuilt in the run is held back (the last good copy stays live) and the run fails, alerting #srs-workflows.\n- **Owner:** fintan.creedon@srsfund.com. Prototype: `exploration/steam/notebooks/build_steam_share.py` (notebook, card checks in `card/`).\n\n## Good to know\n\n- **Fox lags Slice** by about a week and refreshes in bulk, so EU5 and Combined often end earlier than the US.\n- **Fixed merchant cohort** (merchants seen in every month Jan 2021-Oct 2026) moved quarterly share Y/Y by at most 2.4pp, so the page uses all merchants only (analysis in the exploration notebook, section 4.1).\n- **EU before 2021 is noise** (Fox had ~50k active panelists), so charts start in 2021 and Y/Y charts in 2022.\n- **Launch-driven swings.** Q4-25 was lifted by Battlefield 6 and ARC Raiders on Steam (~$1.2m of US Steam spend in 2 Oct-9 Nov 2025), so Q4-26 to date is down Y/Y against that base.\n- **Hardware.** Steam Machine and Steam Frame (2026) add hardware spend; untick Hardware to strip it out.\n- Slice lines are not deduplicated beyond what `ITEMS_EXTENDED` does, consistent with the video-game wallet share in `video_games/notebooks/aaa_franchise_trends.ipynb`.\n",
